<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Greek city-states</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Greek city-states were independent self-governing communities, known as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polis">poleis</a>, such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebes">Thebes</a>, which formed the political framework of ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>. They were brought under Macedonian hegemony by Philip II, a key step in the foundation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/macedonian-empire">Macedonian Empire</a>. Greek cities in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> were also long-standing rivals of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a> and its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punic-empire">Punic Empire</a>.</p></div>
